Flocculation, mixing energy and aggregation (@wu-flocculation: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @water-and-sanitation, #water-and-sanitation "Settling-velocity-surface-loading-and-real-clarifiers.lingua#r_1S2WZCBK4CDNK20DM8MF6CHB4N") { r_2GFMDN32BDXC69JAMT1Y18E56Z
**Read:** [The Physics of Water Treatment Design](../sources/Physics-of-Water-Treatment-Design-AguaClara.lingua#r_1V2KMP22J5KMQF65K8Q7ZXXBAR), chapter 3's [energy dissipation and mixing](https://aguaclara-reach.github.io/Textbook/Fluid_Deformation_and_Energy_Dissipation/FDED_Intro.html) and chapter 7's [flocculation introduction](https://aguaclara-reach.github.io/Textbook/Flocculation/Floc_Intro.html), followed by its derivations and design challenge.

Particles must meet and attach to become useful aggregates, but stronger mixing can also alter or break them. Rapid initial chemical distribution and later aggregation serve different purposes. Treat particle/floc structure, collision and attachment as distinct questions.

A conventional volume-averaged measure is G = √[P/(μV)], where P is power dissipated in the fluid, μ dynamic viscosity and V the mixed volume. Its unit is s⁻¹. In an original idealized example, P = 10 W, μ = 0.001 Pa·s and V = 10 m³ give G ≈ 31.623 s⁻¹. A 600-second nominal interval gives Gt ≈ 18,974, dimensionless. Motor input is not automatically dissipated fluid power, and equal Gt can conceal different local shear histories.

**Work:** Compare 60 seconds at 100 s⁻¹ with 600 seconds at 10 s⁻¹: equal products do not prove equal aggregates or separation. Explain what needs measuring to distinguish collision opportunity, attachment and breakage. Work the book's hydraulic-flocculator challenge, then test peak flow, altered viscosity and short-circuiting. Deepen into population balances, aggregation/breakage kernels and scale dependence; require independent particle or settling evidence rather than inferring every parameter from one turbidity endpoint.

**Water treatment continuation:** [Settling velocity, surface loading and real clarifiers](Settling-velocity-surface-loading-and-real-clarifiers.lingua#r_1S2WZCBK4CDNK20DM8MF6CHB4N).
